                  alt="Illustration showing garment ease and body measurements"
                  class="fit-help-ease__img"
                  width="400"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="fit-help-article__heading">What Do the Fit Choices Mean?</h3>

            <h4 class="fit-help-article__subheading">Close Fit</h4>
            <p>About 1&quot; of ease.</p>
            <p>Fits closer to the body.</p>

            <h4 class="fit-help-article__subheading">Standard Fit</h4>
            <p>About 3&quot; of ease.</p>
            <p>Comfortable everyday fit.</p>

            <h4 class="fit-help-article__subheading">Relaxed Fit</h4>
            <p>About 6&quot; of ease.</p>
            <p>More room and drape.</p>

            <h3 class="fit-help-article__heading">Why Not Just Follow the Model?</h3>
            <p>You have probably seen pattern descriptions that say things like:</p>
            <p class="fit-help-article__quote">
              &ldquo;Model is wearing size Medium with 4 inches of positive ease.&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p>
              But unless you know the finished measurement of the sweater, that information does
              not really help you decide what you will like.
            </p>
            <p>The same sweater can look completely different on two people.</p>

            <h3 class="fit-help-article__heading">Ease Is Personal</h3>
            <p>Some knitters like a close-fitting sweater that follows their shape.</p>
            <p>Others prefer extra room for comfort, layering, or drape.</p>
            <p>Neither choice is right or wrong.</p>

            <h3 class="fit-help-article__heading">The Best Way to Choose</h3>
            <p>
              Instead of guessing, measure a sweater you already own and love to wear.
            </p>
            <p>
              Compare its measurements to your body measurements and you will quickly discover the
              amount of ease that feels best to you.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is one of the easiest ways to create a sweater you will actually enjoy wearing.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
